3","2":"2","3":"4","4":"4","5":"4","6":"5","7":"4","8":"4","9":"4","10":"3","11":"2","12":"3","13":"2","14":"2","15":"5","16":"3","17":"4","18":"4","19":"3","20":"4","21":"5","22":"1","23":"5","24":"2","25":"1","26":"4","27":"1","28":"3","29":"1","30":"2","31":"3","32":"1","33":"1","34":"3","35":"1","36":"3","37":"4","38":"3","39":"1","40":"1"},"issa_status":"Moderate Autism","relating_to_people":"2.5","imitation":"2","emotional_response":"2","body_use":"1.5","object_use":"2.5","adaptation_to_change":"1","visual_response":"2.5","listening_response":"1.5","tst_response_use":"2","fear_nervous":"1.5","verbal":"2.5","non_verbal":"2","activity_level":"2.5","intellectual_response":"2.5","general_imperssions":"2.5","cars_total":"31","cars_status":"Mild-to-Moderate","old_visit_date":"2025-12-06","infants_personal_social":"","infants_cognition":"","infants_cognition_communication":"","infants_fine_motor":"","infants_gross_motor":"","preschoolers_personal_social":"","preschoolers_cognition":"","preschoolers_cognition_communication":"","preschoolers_fine_motor":"","preschoolers_gross_motor":"","cvp_raw_score":"","cvp_developemental_age":"","cvp_rank":"","cvp_development_adaptive_level":"","el_raw_score":"","el_developemental_age":"","el_rank":"","el_development_adaptive_level":"","rl_raw_score":"","rl_developemental_age":"","rl_rank":"","rl_development_adaptive_level":"","fm_raw_score":"","fm_developemental_age":"","fm_rank":"","fm_development_adaptive_level":"","gm_raw_score":"","gm_developemental_age":"","gm_rank":"","gm_development_adaptive_level":"","vmi_raw_score":"","vmi_developemental_age":"","vmi_rank":"","vmi_development_adaptive_level":"","ae_raw_score":"","ae_developemental_age":"","ae_rank":"","ae_development_adaptive_level":"","sr_raw_score":"","sr_developemental_age":"","sr_rank":"","sr_development_adaptive_level":"","cmb_raw_score":"","cmb_developemental_age":"","cmb_rank":"","cmb_development_adaptive_level":"","cvb_raw_score":"","cvb_developemental_age":"","cvb_rank":"","cvb_development_adaptive_level":"","com_rank":"","com_development_adaptive_level":"","com_developemental_age":"","motor_standard_score":"","motor_rank":"","motor_development_adaptive_level":"","motor_developemental_age":"","mb_standard_score":"","mb_rank":"","mb_development_adaptive_level":"","mb_developemental_age":"","file_list":"[]","file_name":"[]","file_size":"[]","visit_id":"1701","baby_background":"<p>Pregnancy: Planned<\/p><p>AN period - Supervised, Uneventful&nbsp;<\/p><p>Peri \/postnatal period: Full term \/ LSCS<\/p><p>Birth Asphyxia - Absent&nbsp;<\/p><p>NICU Stay: Absent; No of Days : 0<\/p><p><strong>Developmental Milestones :<\/strong><\/p><p>Delay: Present<\/p><p>Motor - Walking @ 1 year 6 months<\/p><p>Language: First word - 1 year 3 months<\/p><p>Predominantly language delay<\/p>","recommendation":"<p><br><\/p>","baby_behavior":"<p>On observation, he was hyperactive and restless. He was roaming around the room throughout the session, and it was difficult for the caregiver to make him sit for the assessment and focus on the given activity. Significant inattention was noted. He required frequent breaks and multiple reminders to complete the given task. Eye contact was fleeting. Shrinking of the eye was observed. Response to name-calling was absent. Joint attention was briefly present. Object engagement was predominantly present. <strong>Presymbolic play (doll as agent) was present<\/strong>. Single-finger pointing was present. He had difficulty in comprehending simple commands and attended to the command. Immediate and delayed echolalia were present.&nbsp;&nbsp;<\/p><p><strong>Cognitive (Verbal\/Preverbal)&nbsp;<\/strong><\/p><p>He could indicate the correct placement for the 3 shapes in the form board. He could identify the difference between the 3 noise makers. He had difficulty in selecting objects hidden inside the pouch when named. He was briefly interested in picture books. He had difficulty in pantomiming the appropriate use of five objects. He had difficulty in matching colours and fixing puzzles.<\/p><p><strong>Expressive Language<\/strong><\/p><p>He could say single words or occasionally 2-word combinations with intelligibility above 50%. He could name square and circle. He could use gestures and words to fulfil his needs. He had difficulty with pronouns appropriately. He had difficulty in the use of age-appropriate syntax. He had difficulty using Wh questions.&nbsp;<\/p><p><strong>Receptive Language&nbsp;<\/strong><\/p><p>He could identify body parts, common objects, and pictures from the picture series. He could identify a square and a circle. He had difficulty in identifying action words and following simple commands. He had difficulty in understanding pronouns. Instrumental pointing and single-finger pointing were present.&nbsp;<\/p><p><strong>Fine motor<\/strong>&nbsp;<\/p><p>He could stack up to 6-7 cubes on demonstration. He could remove beads from the string. He could scribble. He had difficulty in tracing shapes and the imitation of vertical lines. Screwing off the lid and blowing bubbles were absent.&nbsp;<\/p><p><strong>Gross motor<\/strong>&nbsp;<\/p><p>The transfer of objects was present. He could throw and kick the ball but had difficulty in catching the ball. He could climb stairs up and down, alternating steps without support. Jumping was present.&nbsp;<\/p><p><strong>Visuomotor Imitation<\/strong><\/p><p>He had difficulty in imitating 3 gross motor movements. Because of his inattention and inattention, he did not attend to further higher-level items in the domain.<\/p>","confidential_background_details":"<p><br><\/p>","diagnosis":"<p>Axis I: Intellectual level : Unspecified developmental delay (Cognition -&nbsp; months) with behaviour problems<\/p><p>Axis II: Etiology : -<\/p><p>Axis III: Medical : -<\/p><p>Axis IV: Psychiatric : Autism spectrum disorder&nbsp;<\/p><p>Axis V: Psychosocial : Expressive screen time present.<\/p>","home_program":"<div class=\"col-md-12 col-sm-12 col-xs-12\"><p class=\"p1\">Encourage <strong>\"Child led play\"<\/strong><\/p><p class=\"p1\">Engage at the&nbsp;<strong>\"Child's Eye Level\"<\/strong><\/p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Give more oppurtunity<\/strong><\/p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Sitting tolerance :<\/strong><\/p><p class=\"p1\">Sit and Play along with your child.<\/p><p class=\"p1\">Have a set of favourite toys. Gain his interest in toys.<\/p><p class=\"p1\">Engage him with his favourite toys<\/p><\/div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto;\" data-mce-style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto;\"><strong>Eye Contact and Joint attention<\/strong><\/p><ul type=\"disc\"><li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\" data-mce-style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\">To focus on EYE CONTACT for each small household activity \/ when playing with the child.<\/li><li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\" data-mce-style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\">Encourage more social games like peek - a - boo, tickle games at his level. Wait for him to respond by looking at your eyes and then play again.&nbsp;<\/li><li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\" data-mce-style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\">CALL, LOOK, COME, GO, TAKE, GIVE, SHOW, BRING and POINT. These are the activities to enhance joint attention, i.e., the child and mother focusing their attention on an object and then doing things together.<\/li><li class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\" data-mce-style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; mso-margin-bottom-alt: auto; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1; tab-stops: list 36.0pt;\">In all daily activities like eating, bathing etc. \"Look at the soap, see foam !!! Wow...\" Show it - make him look, then ask him - make him point<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Cognitive Verbal\/Preverbal (CVP)&nbsp;<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Object permanence activities - hide a favourite toy or object of the child under a cup and encourage the child to find it.<\/li><li>Encourage copying lines and shapes (vertical lines, circle, square, triangle and diamond).<\/li><li>Encourage of 2 - piece puzzles<\/li><li>Teach him to identify and match 2 shapes - circle and square - Play using form boards.<\/li><li>Teach him to identify and sort colours (Primary colours - Red and yellow)<\/li><\/ul><p><strong style=\"font-size: 12pt;\" data-mce-style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Receptive Language&nbsp;<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Response to name call - consistently<\/li><li>Encourage identifying big and little sizes.<\/li><li>Encourage the identification action words<\/li><li>Encourage following commands.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong style=\"font-size: 12pt;\" data-mce-style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Expressive Language<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Improve vocabulary using flashcards (fruits, vegetables, animals, vechicles)<\/li><li>Encourage naming action words<\/li><li>Encourage 2 word combination<\/li><li>Encourage naming colours<\/li><li>Encourage role-playing with puppets<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Fine Motor&nbsp;<\/strong><\/p><ul><li class=\"p1\">Encourage building blocks\/staking<\/li><li class=\"p1\">Encourage scribbling and imitating strokes \u2013 focus on grasp<br><\/li><li>Encourage stringing beads \u2013 consistently<br><\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Gross Motor&nbsp;<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Catching, throwing and kicking the ball - goal directed&nbsp;<\/li><li>Encourage hopping.<\/li><li>Encourage playing a simple game with rules.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Visual motor Imitation<\/strong><\/p><ul><li>Encourage imitation of gross motor activites&nbsp;<\/li><li>Encourage imitation in day-to-day activities.&nbsp;<br><\/li><\/ul><p>Plan<\/p><p>Requires intensive Occupational therapy and speech therapy.<\/p>","UserModified":53,"DateModified":{"date":"2025-12-15 12:11:37.888374","timezone_type":3,"timezone":"Asia\/Kolkata"},"BirthStatus":"Outborn"}  
